== Geography ==
Giewont lies in the area of the Polish Tatra National Park (Tatrzański Park Narodowy). It encompasses three peaks: Small Giewont (Polish: Mały Giewont, 1,728 metres or 5,669 feet), Great Giewont (Wielki Giewont, 1,894 metres or 6,214 feet) and Long Giewont (Długi Giewont, 1,867 metres or 6,125 feet). There is a mountain pass located between Great and Long Giewont, known as Szczerba (1,823 metres or 5,981 feet). It is located between the valleys of Kondratowa (a branch of the Bystra Valley), Małej Łąki and Strążyska. The 600-metre-high (2,000 ft) northern face of Great Giewont is clearly visible from the nearby town of Zakopane and remains one of the most characteristic features of the panorama...","coordinates":[{"lat":49.25055556,"lon":19.93388889,"primary":true,"globe":"earth"}],"pageimage":"Giewont_T58.jpg","pageprops":{"page_image_free":"Giewont_T58.jpg","wikibase-shortdesc":"Mountain in Poland","wikibase_item":"Q1523423"}}]}}
